DATE_FORMAT()

DATE_FORMAT() is used to display a date in the format you want.

It doesn't change the actual date stored in the database. It only changes how the date appears in the query result.

Basic syntax
DATE_FORMAT(date, format)

For example:

SELECT DATE_FORMAT('2024-01-10', '%d-%m-%Y');

Result:

10-01-2024
🔑 Important format symbols

These are the ones you should remember first:

Symbol	Meaning	Example
%Y	4-digit year	2024
%y	2-digit year	24
%m	Month number	01
%d	Day	10
%M	Full month name	January
%b	Short month name	Jan
%W	Full weekday name	Wednesday
%a	Short weekday name	Wed
Example

Your date:

2024-01-10

Can be displayed as:

DATE_FORMAT(joining_date, '%d-%m-%Y')

→

10-01-2024

Or:

DATE_FORMAT(joining_date, '%d %M %Y')

→

10 January 2024

Or:

DATE_FORMAT(joining_date, '%M %d, %Y')

→

January 10, 2024
🔥 Using YOUR table
SELECT emp_name,
       DATE_FORMAT(joining_date, '%d-%m-%Y') AS formatted_date
FROM employees;

Your database still stores:

2024-01-10

We're just displaying it as:

10-01-2024
